WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.752
Attention is only one sublayer inside a Transformer block.

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:10.072
After multi-head attention produces an updated vector for each token, the model keeps a path back to the

00:00:10.072 --> 00:00:13.277
original input through a residual connection.

00:00:13.277 --> 00:00:19.782
In a common arrangement, we add the attention output to the input and apply layer normalization.

00:00:19.782 --> 00:00:24.172
The result then passes through a position-wise feed-forward network,

00:00:24.172 --> 00:00:29.047
usually two linear transformations with a nonlinear activation between them.

00:00:29.047 --> 00:00:33.137
Another residual connection and normalization step follow.

00:00:33.137 --> 00:00:35.852
Why do we need these extra pieces?

00:00:35.852 --> 00:00:39.542
Attention mixes information across token positions.

00:00:39.542 --> 00:00:44.302
The feed-forward network transforms the features within each token vector.

00:00:44.302 --> 00:00:49.822
Residual connections help information and gradients travel through a deep stack of layers.

00:00:49.822 --> 00:00:53.722
Normalization helps keep the scale of activations stable.

00:00:53.722 --> 00:00:58.202
Different Transformer families may order normalization slightly differently,

00:00:58.202 --> 00:01:01.692
but this combination of attention, residual paths, normalization,

00:01:01.692 --> 00:01:07.367
and feed-forward transformation is the repeating unit we mean by a Transformer block.
